= Language =

== Overview ==
'''Language''' is a structured system used in human communication and cognition. Human languages can be spoken or signed, and many also have written forms.

Linguistics studies language at several interacting levels, including sound or sign structure, word formation, [[Syntax|syntax]], [[Semantics|semantics]], [[Pragmatics|pragmatics]], discourse, variation, acquisition, processing, and historical change.

There is no single theory-neutral answer to what a language ultimately ''is''. Different traditions emphasize social conventions, individual cognitive capacities, abstract structural systems, patterns of use, or combinations of these.

== Modalities ==
Human language is not identical to speech.

'''Spoken languages''' use vocal-auditory signals.

'''Signed languages''' use visual-manual and bodily signals and possess rich phonological, morphological, syntactic, and semantic structure.

'''Writing''' represents language through graphic systems, but many languages have existed without standardized writing.

The existence of signed languages makes the distinction especially important:

<pre>
language ≠ speech
language ≠ writing
</pre>

== Structure ==
Languages exhibit organization at multiple scales.

Common areas of analysis include:

* '''phonetics''' — physical production and perception of speech/sign;
* '''phonology''' — patterned contrastive organization;
* '''morphology''' — internal structure of words or signs;
* '''[[Syntax]]''' — combination into larger expressions;
* '''[[Semantics]]''' — conventional meaning and interpretation;
* '''[[Pragmatics]]''' — interpretation in use and context;
* '''[[Discourse (linguistics)|discourse]]''' — connected language use beyond isolated expressions.

These divisions are analytical tools. Real linguistic phenomena frequently cross their boundaries.

== Productivity ==
Human language is productive: finite speakers can produce and understand novel expressions.

Part of this capacity comes from systematic combination. [[Compositionality]] describes the influential idea that the interpretation of complex expressions depends systematically on their parts and structure.

Productivity does not mean that every expression is predictable word by word. Idioms, conventional constructions, [[Ambiguity|ambiguity]], [[Polysemy|polysemy]], and contextual interpretation all complicate simple accounts.

== Language and meaning ==
Meaning operates at several levels.

[[Lexical semantics]] studies meaning associated with lexical items.

Sentence interpretation depends on lexical content and syntactic structure.

[[Pragmatics]] studies how utterance interpretation depends on [[Context (linguistics)|context]], intentions, [[Common ground|common ground]], and communicative practices.

The boundary between semantic and pragmatic contribution is theoretically disputed.

== Language as social and cognitive phenomenon ==
Language is simultaneously observable in interaction and supported by human cognitive capacities.

Researchers therefore study:

* community conventions and variation;
* individual knowledge and processing;
* acquisition and development;
* social identity and interaction;
* historical change;
* typological similarities and differences across languages.

Different theoretical frameworks assign different explanatory priority to these dimensions.

== Natural and formal languages ==
A '''natural language''' develops and changes through human communities and cognition.

A '''formal language''' is defined by explicit symbolic rules for a technical purpose.

Programming languages, logical calculi, and data languages can borrow terms such as syntax and semantics from linguistics, but they are not natural languages merely because they contain symbols and rules.

== Epistemic notes ==
Language is an established subject of empirical and theoretical study, but its ontology is contested. A public article should not silently identify "language" with only a grammar, a dictionary, a set of sentences, an internal brain state, or a social convention.

Claims about universals, innate structure, learning mechanisms, and the boundaries between language and other forms of communication remain active areas of research.

